## Decision

We will implement a custom warning utility that wraps `console.warn()` and conditionally compiles based on the `NODE_ENV` environment variable.

For development environments (`NODE_ENV === 'development'`), the utility will emit warnings to the console using `console.warn()`. This allows developers to receive immediate feedback about deprecations, unsupported features, or other important messages during development.

For production environments (`NODE_ENV === 'production'`), the utility will be configured to be entirely removed from the compiled bundle during the build process. This ensures that no `console.warn()` calls or related development-specific code are included in production builds, preventing unnecessary code bloat and potential performance impacts.

This approach will likely involve a build-time configuration (e.g., using Webpack's `DefinePlugin` or similar tools for other bundlers) to replace the warning utility's implementation with a no-op during production builds.

### Example (Conceptual)

```javascript
// src/utils/warn.js

if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development') {
  module.exports = function warn(message) {
    console.warn(`[Primer React] ${message}`);
  };
} else {
  // In production, this function will be completely optimized out by bundlers
  // configured to handle `process.env.NODE_ENV`
  module.exports = function warn() {};
}
```

```javascript
// src/components/MyComponent.js
import warn from '../utils/warn';

function MyComponent({ deprecatedProp }) {
  if (deprecatedProp) {
    warn('The "deprecatedProp" prop is deprecated and will be removed in a future version.');
  }
  // ... component logic
}
```

During the build process for production, the bundler will see `process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'`, and therefore the `warn` function will resolve to the empty function `function () {}`. Furthermore, advanced bundlers and minifiers are often able to entirely eliminate the dead code path (the `if` block and the `warn` call itself) when the `warn` function becomes a no-op, resulting in zero bytes of warning-related code in production.

